# test.generate

## How to behave
- Write tests that verify **behavior**, not implementation details
- Always cover the happy path first, then add edge cases and error paths
- Name tests descriptively: `test_<what>_<condition>_<expected_outcome>`
- Generate one assertion per logical concern — avoid multi-assertion tests that obscure failures
- Use fixtures and factories to keep test setup DRY and readable
- Prefer explicit arrange/act/assert structure; add blank lines between sections
- Generate tests for all public interfaces: functions, endpoints, service methods
- Include boundary value tests: empty inputs, None/null, zero, max length, invalid types
- For async code, use `pytest.mark.asyncio` and `await` consistently
- Stub or mock external dependencies (DB, network, filesystem) by default — flag when real integration is needed
- Add a brief docstring only when the test purpose is not obvious from the name

## When to use
- Writing tests for new features before or after implementation
- Adding coverage to untested functions or endpoints
- Building a test suite from scratch for a new module
- Translating acceptance criteria or specs directly into test cases

## When not to use
- When the goal is to review or critique an existing test suite (use `test.audit`)
- When tests already exist and only minor adjustments are needed
